From: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_core: skb reuse: test zerocopy in the right field, keep
 page_pool skbs

The reuse path hands a transmitted skb back to the firmware as an RX
buffer when nothing else can still reach it. Two of its checks were
wrong:

- the zerocopy test read SKBFL_ZEROCOPY_ENABLE from shinfo->tx_flags,
  while the SKBFL_* bits live in shinfo->flags; skb_zcopy() reads the
  right field. It only held because zerocopy skbs are also nonlinear,
  which a later check rejects.
- an skb whose head belongs to a page_pool (pp_recycle) goes back to
  its pool on free; reusing it cleared pp_recycle and leaked the page
  from the pool for good. Ethernet drivers with page_pool RX (stmmac
  among them) hand the stack exactly such skbs.

Signed-off-by: Andrei-Alexandru Bleortu <me@andrei-z.com>
---
--- a/nss_core.c
+++ b/nss_core.c
@@ -2771,7 +2771,7 @@ static inline bool nss_core_skb_can_reus
 
 	if (unlikely(irqs_disabled()))
 		return false;
-	if (unlikely(skb_shinfo(nbuf)->tx_flags & SKBFL_ZEROCOPY_ENABLE))
+	if (unlikely(skb_zcopy(nbuf)))
 		return false;
 
 	if (unlikely(skb_is_nonlinear(nbuf)))
@@ -2800,6 +2800,13 @@ static inline bool nss_core_skb_can_reus
 		return false;
 
 	/*
+	 * A page_pool head goes back to its pool on free; reuse would clear
+	 * pp_recycle and leak it from the pool.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(nbuf->pp_recycle))
+		return false;
+
+	/*
 	 * TODO: This check is only validated on kernel 6.6
 	 * This needs to be validated on prior linux
 	 * kernel versions.
